      <workList xml:id="workDesc_N203FD">
         <work xml:id="work_d1e222219">
            <identifier label="SchW" xml:id="identifier_N20404">A2:007</identifier>
            <title xml:id="title_d30f5740" xml:lang="it">Sinfonia ex B♯ </title>
            <contributor xml:id="respStmt_N20411">
               <persName role="composer" xml:id="persName_2ee54a3f">Johann Adolph Scheibe</persName>
            </contributor>
            <history xml:id="history_8c6a6abc">
               <p xml:id="idm209">The present sinfonia for strings has survived in two exemplars – both located in Sweden. The provenance of the sources suggests that Scheibe's sinfonias in a light style were popular among musical amateurs. The instrumental material in Vexjö was produced by the little known composer, musician and copyist <persName xml:id="idm210" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Johann Christian Zschotzscher</persName>, who was active in the second half of the eighteenth century. The material in Lund was most likely part of the musical society's collection (Lunds Akademiska kapellet) which was founded in 1745 and whose first director of music was <persName xml:id="idm211" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Friedrich Kraus</persName> (1724–1780). The material might have been copied from material in the royal court's music archives in Stockholm 1754–55, or perhaps even obtained directly from Scheibe.</p>
            </history>
